rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Number, size, time and date printing (into the output buffer, OUTBUF_CHAR)

@311A SIZEK_SHIFT_LP
  One pass of the size-in-K printer begun at $3118: shifts the 4-byte number whose top byte
  is at HL one bit right; C counts the 10 passes (a divide by 1024).
  .311A Keep the top byte's address
  .311B B = 4 bytes
  .311D Clear carry: a 0 enters at the top

@311E SIZEK_SHR_BYTE
  Shifts one byte of the number right through carry, top byte first; after 10 passes prints
  the quotient (PRINT_DEC32, no padding) and a "K".
  .3123 HL = the top byte again
  .3124 10 passes done?
  .3127 DE = the number's first byte, pushed by the caller at $30D3
  .3128 A = 0: no padding

@312C OUT_K
  Writes "K" to the output buffer (a tail jump to OUTBUF_CHAR).
  Entry: IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: as OUTBUF_CHAR: A = 0 and Z, or EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.

@312E OUT_A_TAIL
  Writes A to the output buffer: a shared tail jump to OUTBUF_CHAR.

@3131 PRINT_TIME
  Writes a space and the time held in a directory entry's time word (hours in bits 15-11,
  minutes in bits 10-5) as "hh:mm". With bit 7 of IY-$4F clear the hours run 12-11 and an "a"
  or "p" follows; with it set they run 0-23.
  The minute bits are shifted out of the word in place: the entry's time word is changed.
  Entry: HL = the time word's high byte (its low byte at HL-1); IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z.
  Error: NZ, A = EXOS's status when a full buffer could not be written.
  Changes: AF, B, DE, HL; the time word.
  Keeps: C, IX, IY.
  .3136 B = 0: no suffix yet
  .3137 A = the hours (bits 7-3 of the high byte)
  .313D 24-hour clock?
  .3143 B = "a"
  .3145 Afternoon?
  .3149 Hours 12-23 become 0-11
  .314B B = "p"

@314D TIME_HOUR_ZERO
  Hour 0 of a 12-hour clock is shown as 12.
  .314D Hour 0?
  .3150 Then 12

@3152 TIME_PRINT_HM
  Writes the hours as two digits, a colon, the minutes as two digits and, for the 12-hour
  clock, the "a" or "p" kept in B.
  .3155 ":"
  .315A A = the high byte; the low byte's top 3 bits are rotated in below
  .315B HL = the low byte
  .3165 A = the minutes
  .316A B = 0 (24-hour clock)?

@316D OUT_A_SHORT
  Writes A to the output buffer (on to OUT_A_TAIL).

@316F PRINT_DATE
  Writes a space and the date held in a directory entry's date word (year - 1980 in bits
  15-9, month in bits 8-5, day in bits 4-0) as three two-digit fields joined by "-". The order
  comes from bits 5-0 of IY-$4F, two bits per field (1 = day, 2 = month, 3 = year); 0 there
  means day-month-year ($6C). The year is shown as its last two digits.
  Entry: HL = the date word (low byte first); IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: NC; A = the format byte rotated.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .316F C = the day
  .3175 H = the year - 1980 (bit 8 of the word goes to carry)
  .3178 A = the month, its top bit from carry
  .317E L = the month
  .317F A = the field order, moved to bits 7-2
  .3184 Order set?
  .3186 Else day, month, year

@3188 DATE_FIELDS
  Three fields, the first one after a space.
  .3188 B = 3 fields
  .318A E = " ", the separator before the first field

@318C DATE_FIELD_LP
  Takes the next field's two bits from the top of A and picks its value: day (C), month (L)
  or year (H + 80).
  .318C Next two bits to the bottom
  .318E Keep the rest for the next field
  .318F D = the field's selector
  .3192 Selector 0: the value is 0
  .3194 Selector 1: the day
  .3198 Selector 2: the month
  .319C Selector 3: the year
  .319D Years count from 1980

@319F DATE_PUT_FIELD
  Writes the separator, then the field's value below 100.
  .319F D = the value
  .31A0 Separator first
  .31A5 From 2000 on?
  .31A9 Two digits only

@31AB DATE_NEXT_FIELD
  Writes the value as two digits; the next field is preceded by "-".
  .31AE A = the remaining selectors
  .31AF E = "-" from the second field on
  .31B3 NC

@31B5 PRINT_2DIGITS
  Writes A (0-99) as two decimal digits.
  Entry: A = the value; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z.
  Error: NZ, A = EXOS's status when a full buffer could not be written.
  Changes: AF, DE.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .31B5 E = "0" - 1

@31B7 TWODIG_TENS_LP
  Counts the tens in E, writes them, then the units.
  .31B7 Take off 10
  .31B9 One more ten
  .31BC D = the units - 10
  .31BD The tens digit
  .31C2 The units digit: units - 10 + "0" + 10

@31F4 PRINT_DE_DEC
  Writes DE in decimal without padding, building the 4-byte number in the 4 bytes that end at
  HL (the caller's $31E6 and OUTBUF_ERRTEXT enter here).
  Entry: DE = the value; HL = the last of 4 free bytes; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: as PRINT_DEC32.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, DE', L'; the 4 bytes.
  .31F4 The high word is 0
  .31F9 The low word is DE
  .31FC DE = the number; A = 0: no padding

@31FD PRINT_DEC32
  Writes the 4-byte number at DE in decimal, by subtracting the powers of ten in the table at
  $3233 ($FF ends it): each power's count is one digit. Leading zeros are left out when A is
  0, or written as A (a space right-aligns the number in 10 columns); the units digit always
  shows. The number is used up: it is 0 at the end.
  Entry: A = the padding character, 0 for none; DE = the number (low byte first); IY =
  EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z.
  Changes: AF, BC, HL, DE', L'; the number.
  Keeps: DE, IX, IY.
  .31FE E' = the padding character
  .31FF D' = "0"
  .3202 HL = the powers of ten, 1000000000 first

@3205 DEC32_POWER_LP
  The next power; from the last one (1) on, a zero is written as "0".
  .3205 The power 1?
  .3209 Then the units digit always shows

@320C DEC32_DIGIT
  C counts the subtractions; carry is clear for the first.
  .320C C = 0, NC

@320E DEC32_SUB_LP
  Subtracts the power once (4 bytes).
  .320E Keep the power and the number
  .3210 B = 4 bytes

@3212 DEC32_SUB_BYTE
  Subtracts byte by byte until the number goes below 0, then adds the power back (ADD32,
  which also moves HL to the next power), and writes the digit: C - 1, through E' while
  it is 0. A JR into $3228 runs its operand ($B3) as OR E: the zero digit's character.
  .3212 Number byte - power byte - borrow
  .3219 Back to the number's first byte
  .321A Back to the power's first byte
  .321B One more subtraction
  .321C Not below 0 yet?
  .321E Add the power back; HL = the next power
  .3221 A = the count
  .3223 Digit 0?
  .3226 A = the digit's character
  .3227 Every later 0 is written as "0"
  .3228 Its operand ($B3), entered by JR: OR E, the zero's character or 0
  .322A Write the digit, unless a leading zero is dropped
  .322E The $FF end mark?

@3254 ADD32
  Adds the 4-byte number at HL to the one at DE (low bytes first).
  Entry: DE = the sum; HL = the number to add.
  Exit: the sum at DE; HL = HL + 4; B = 0; carry = the carry out of the top byte.
  Changes: AF, B, HL; the sum.
  Keeps: C, DE, IX, IY.
  .3255 B = 4 bytes

@3258 ADD32_LOOP
  Adds one byte with carry.

@3263 KBD_CHAN_TRY
  Tries channel H: EXOS 11 special function 9; channels up to $FF are tried.
  .3264 B = 9 (@@JOY), C = 0
  .3269 It answered: Z, H = the channel
  .326A Next channel
  .326D None: H = $FF, NZ

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Command-line helpers: argument parsing and EXDOS function calls on the request block

@3BFC NEED_PARAM
  Checks that the parse found something (B bit 0 or 2 set): the error otherwise is $9F
  (Wrong number of parameters).
  Entry: B = the parse's flags.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, NC.
  Error: A = $9F, NZ.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .3BFD Anything given?
  .3C01 A = 0 when it was, else $FF
  .3C02 A = 0 or $9F

@3C07 ARGS_END
  Checks that only spaces and tabs are left after an argument: C is the character that ended
  it and DE points at it; the code from $3C08 steps over spaces and tabs to the next one. A 0
  ends the command line. $3C05 enters with Z to check C first.
  Entry: C = the character; DE = its address.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z, NC.
  Error: A = $9F (Wrong number of parameters), NZ.
  Changes: AF, C, DE.
  Keeps: B, HL, IX, IY.
  .3C07 A = the character

@3C1F FIND_FIRST_ARG
  Starts the search for the request block's name (EXDOS function 4) and returns the first
  entry its attributes allow. The search's return goes through $3CDC (pushed first), which
  filters by attribute ($3D61) and goes on with function 5 until an entry passes, counting
  the entries found at IY-$28.
  1. In directory mode (carry kept in F' by PARSE_ARG_MODE, see $3C18) bit 4 of IY-$2A is set
     and the search's result returned as it is.
  2. Else, when a single name (B bit 5 clear) was found and it is a directory (entry
     attribute bit 4, IX+$43), the name is appended to the path text at IY+$135 ($15DB) and
     the path's text is cut there.
  3. When the output buffer already holds text ("Directory of "), the drive and path are
     written after it (PRINT_DRV_PATH).
  4. For a directory found in step 2 the result becomes the request block ($3CBA, the name
     all "?"), bit 5 of IX+$13 is set and the search starts again inside it (FN4_FIND).
  Entry: B = the parse's flags; IX = the request block; F' carry = directory mode; IY =
  EXDOS's base.
  Exit: NC, A = 0 and Z; the entry's descriptor at IX+$30 (BC); DE = IX.
  Error: C with A = $CF when nothing matched, or A = the error code; C with A = 0 when the
  search ran out after some matches.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'; IY-$28, IY-$27.
  Keeps: IX.
  .3C1F The search's return: the attribute filter at $3CDC
  .3C23 No entry found yet
  .3C2A DE = the request block
  .3C2E BC = the result buffer
  .3C31 Function 4: find the first entry
  .3C34 Directory mode?
  .3C38 Keep the result
  .3C39 HL = the path text the search returns
  .3C3A A = the output buffer's count
  .3C3E DE = the path text
  .3C3F HL = the kept result (H = the status)
  .3C41 C = the count of characters waiting
  .3C42 Found?
  .3C46 The name was all "?" (none given)?
  .3C4A A directory?
  .3C50 Append the directory's name to the path at IY+$135
  .3C59 HL' = the path text; find its 0 end
  .3C5C Next character

@3C83 FN4_FIND
  Runs EXDOS function 4 (find the first entry) through EXDOS_FN_CALL.
  Entry: DE = the request block; BC = its result buffer; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: as EXDOS_FN_CALL.
  Error: as EXDOS_FN_CALL.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.
  Keeps: IX.
  .3C83 Function 4

@3C85 EXDOS_FN_CALL
  Runs EXDOS function A through FUNC_DISPATCH, keeping IX.
  Entry: A = the function; BC, DE, HL its parameters; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z, NC; BC, DE, HL as the function left them.
  Error: C and NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.
  Keeps: IX.
  .3C8C Done?
  .3C8D C: an error

@3C8F PARSE_ARG_MODE
  PARSE_ARG for a command that may work on directories: carry on entry (RD, MVDIR, RNDIR)
  sets bit 4 of IY-$2B (directories only); A and the flags are kept in AF' for the command
  ($3C95).
  Entry: AF = the command's mode; C = function 1's flags; HL = the text; IX = the request
  block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: as PARSE_ARG; AF' = the entry AF.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF'.
  Keeps: IX.
  .3C8F Directories?
  .3C91 Only directories

@3C96 PARSE_ARG
  Takes an argument apart into the request block (EXDOS function 1, FN_PARSE_SPEC). When B
  comes back with bits 3 and 4 clear (no name in it), the block's name (+8) is filled with 11
  "?" and B bit 5 is set ($3CA0).
  Entry: HL = the argument text; C = function 1's flags (bit 0 = do not copy the name); IX =
  the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; B = the parse's flags; C = the character that ended the path, DE its
  address; HL kept.
  Error: C and NZ, A = the parse's error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, IY.
  Keeps: HL, IX.
  .3C96 Function 1: parse the specification
  .3C9B A name given?

@3CD4 FIND_NEXT_ARG
  Goes on with the search (EXDOS function 5 from $3CD7) to the next entry that passes the
  attribute filter, as FIND_FIRST_ARG's return does.
  Entry: IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: as FIND_FIRST_ARG.
  Error: as FIND_FIRST_ARG.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.
  Keeps: IX.
  .3CD4 BC = the result buffer, DE = the block

@3CFF IX_RESULT_BUF
  DE = the request block (IX), BC = its result buffer (IX+$30).
  Entry: IX = the request block.
  Exit: DE = IX; BC = IX+$30.
  Changes: F, BC, DE.
  Keeps: A, HL, IX, IY.

@3D0B NEXT_REQ_BLOCK
  Moves IX to the next request block ($60 bytes on) and prepares it (the code from $3D10, the
  one the command dispatcher runs at $2C47): flags (+6) and drive (+7) = A, first byte A - 1.
  Entry: A = the value (0); IX = the request block.
  Exit: IX = DE = the new block; C = A; A = A - 1.
  Changes: AF, C, DE, IX.
  .3D0B The next block is $60 bytes on

@3D1D PARSE_NAME_OPTS
  Takes the first argument apart (PARSE_ARG_MODE), requires one (NEED_PARAM) and reads the
  "/H" option ($3D3B with the table at $33BB).
  Entry: AF = the command's mode (carry = directories); C = function 1's flags; HL = the text;
  IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; B = the parse's flags.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code ($9F with no argument).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  .3D24 The options: "/H" only

@3D2C OPTS_BY_KIND
  Reads the options from the table at HL for a file operand (carry), else from the table at
  HL + 4 (the options a channel or device may take); then $3D3B.
  Entry: carry = a file operand; HL = the option table.
  Exit: as $3D3B.
  Changes: AF, C, DE, HL, C'.
  .3D2C A file: every option
  .3D2E Else the table's later part

@3D92 SKIP_BLANKS
  Steps HL over spaces and tabs (at most 255).
  Entry: HL = the text.
  Exit: HL = the first other character, A = it; Z when it is the 0 end; NC.
  Changes: AF, C, HL.
  Keeps: B, DE, IX, IY.
  .3D92 C counts down from 255

@3DA3 READ_DECIMAL
  Reads the decimal digits at HL into C (modulo 256).
  Entry: HL = the text.
  Exit: C = the number; HL = the first non-digit; carry set.
  Changes: AF, C, HL.
  Keeps: B, DE, IX, IY.
  .3DA3 C = 0

@3DEC PARSE_CHAN_NUM
  Reads a channel number written "#n" or "#n:".
  Entry: HL = the "#".
  Exit: DE = the channel number; A = 0, Z; HL after the number (and its colon).
  Changes: AF, C, DE, HL.
  .3DEC Over the "#"
  .3DF0 A colon after it?

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The commands (the table at $2C58). The dispatcher enters each with HL = the argument text,
# IX = DE = the request block (IY+$FD), C = 0 and A = $FF (NZ, NC).

@327F CMD_CD
  The CD and CHDIR commands: with a path, makes it the current directory (EXDOS function 2,
  CHANGE_DIR); without one, writes the drive's current directory (function 3, GET_CUR_DIR)
  as "A:\path" and a new line.
  Entry: HL = the argument text; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code ($9F for a second argument).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .327F C bit 0: the last element is entered too, its name not copied
  .3284 Nothing after it?
  .3288 A path given?
  .328A Function 2: make it the current directory
  .328F B = the drive
  .3292 Function 3: the current directory's text
  .3298 The text is followed by a new line
  .329C DE = the path text

@329D PRINT_DRV_PATH
  Writes the request block's drive (IX+7) as "A:", the path separator and the path text at DE.
  Entry: DE = the path text; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z; C = the path's first character.
  Error: NZ, A = EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, C, DE.
  Keeps: B, HL, IX, IY.
  .32A0 "\" or "'", by the language

@32AB CMD_DIR
  The DIR command: lists the files matching the argument (all with none), with the "/W"
  (wide: names only, six to a line) and "/H" (hidden files too) options.
  1. Takes the argument apart and reads the options (IY-$2A: bit 7 /W, bit 1 /H).
  2. Writes the volume line (VOL_LABEL), then "Directory of " and the path (FIND_FIRST_ARG).
  3. Each entry: its name and, in the long form, "h"/"r" marks, the size (or "<dir>"), the
     date and the time; the sizes are added up at IX-$123 (IY-$26).
  4. The summary: the total size, " in ", the number of files, and the drive's free space
     in K (EXDOS function 21).
  Entry: HL = the argument text; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .32B0 The options: "/W" and "/H"
  .32B6 Nothing else after them?
  .32BB An empty path text: the root
  .32BE Read the name raw
  .32C2 The volume line

@32C9 DIR_EXIT_POP
  The end of DIR's start: drops the kept BC and HL; an error returns.

@32CA DIR_EXIT_HL
  Drops the kept HL (or the kept result) and returns on an error.

@32D6 DIR_HEADER
  C' = 0 (the wide form's column), then "Directory of " and the first entry.
  .32D6 Column 0
  .32D8 "Directory of "
  .32DD The first entry, and the path after the text
  .32E0 Found one?
  .32E3 $CF (nothing found) is not an error here

@32E8 DIR_LISTING
  Ends the header line with a blank line and clears the total size (IX-$123).
  .32E8 F' carry: nothing found
  .32E9 End the line
  .32EC And a blank line
  .32F0 HL = the total size (4 bytes)

@32FC DIR_ENTRY_LP
  One entry: its name, then the wide or the long form; the entry's size is added to the
  total.
  .32FC No more entries?
  .32FE Z: the long form (/W not given)
  .3305 The name: padded columns, or compact with /W
  .3308 DE = the entry's size (descriptor +$24)
  .330E DE = the total
  .3313 Add the size to the total
  .3316 DE = the size
  .3317 /W?
  .331D C = the entry's attributes
  .3320 Read-only?
  .3325 "h" when hidden, else a space
  .332C "r" when read-only
  .3334 A directory?
  .333A The size in 10 columns
  .333F Z: no "<dir>"

@3340 DIR_SIZE_DATE
  Writes "<dir>" for a directory, then the entry's date and time and a new line.
  .3340 " <dir>  "
  .3347 HL = the date word (entry +$18)
  .334C The date
  .3350 HL = the time word's high byte
  .3351 The time

@335A DIR_WIDE_COL
  The wide form: counts the columns in C' and starts a new line after six names.
  .335B One more column
  .335D Six on the line?
  .3361 Column 0 again

@3366 DIR_WIDE_NEXT
  Back to the main registers.

@3367 DIR_NEXT
  Finds the next entry and loops.

@336C DIR_SUMMARY
  Ends the listing: a new line after a part-filled wide line, then the total size, " in ",
  the file count ("file" or "files"), and the free space: function 21's free clusters times
  the sectors per cluster, halved (512-byte sectors), in K with " free".
  .336C An error ended the search?
  .336E A partly filled wide line?
  .3374 A space
  .3377 HL = the number of files
  .3378 HL = the total size
  .337C The total, in bytes or K
  .337F " in "
  .3385 The count and " file", with its plural
  .338A Four spaces
  .338F B = the drive
  .3392 Function 21: the free space
  .3398 A = $29 (unused); a JR into its operand runs ADD HL,HL
  .339A Shift once more for each power of 2 in the sectors per cluster
  .339E Halve: sectors to K
  .33A2 The free space
  .33A5 "K"
  .33A8 " free"

@33BE CMD_RD
  The RD and RMDIR commands: DEL for directories (carry set).
  Entry: as CMD_DEL.
  Exit: as CMD_DEL.
  Error: as CMD_DEL.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .33BE Carry: directories

@33BF CMD_DEL
  The DEL, ERA and ERASE commands (and RD): deletes every matching entry (EXDOS function 8),
  read-only ones left out; the "/H" option takes hidden ones too. A name of all "?" asks
  "Erase all files (Y/N)?" first.
  Entry: AF = the mode (carry for directories); HL = the argument text; IX = the request
  block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, C after the last one.
  Error: C and NZ, A = the error code ($CF when nothing matched).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .33BF The name and "/H"
  .33C2 Nothing else after them?
  .33C6 Read-only entries are not taken
  .33CA The first entry
  .33CE HL = the name (+8)
  .33D2 B = 11 characters
  .33D4 "?"

@33D6 DEL_ALL_Q_LP
  Is every character of the name "?"; then asks first.
  .33D6 "?" here?
  .33DC "Erase all files"
  .33E1 Ask; not "yes" returns

@33E5 DEL_START
  BC = the result buffer.

@33E8 DEL_LOOP
  Deletes the entry found, then finds the next.
  .33E8 HL = the request block
  .33E9 DE = the entry's descriptor
  .33EB Function 8: delete
  .33F0 Then the next one
  .33F3 Until the search ends

@33F6 YES_NO_REPLY
  Writes " (Y/N)? ", writes out the buffer and reads a line ($2FE2); the answer is yes when it
  is one character, the yes letter: "J" when EXOS variable $90 is 0 (German), else "Y".
  Entry: the question in the output buffer; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0; Z for yes, NZ for anything else.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  .33F6 " (Y/N)? "
  .33FE One character?
  .3403 In capitals
  .3407 Read EXOS variable $90
  .340D D = its value
  .340E "J"
  .3410 German?
  .3414 Else "Y"

@3416 YN_COMPARE
  The answer against the yes letter.
  .3417 Yes?

@3418 YN_EXIT_A0
  A = 0, the flags kept.

@341B CMD_MD
  The MD and MKDIR commands: makes a directory (EXDOS function 7, MAKE_DIR).
  Entry: HL = the argument text; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code ($9F without a name or with a second argument).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .341E A name given?
  .3422 Nothing after it?
  .342A Function 7: make the directory

@342F CMD_MVDIR
  The MVDIR command: MOVE for directories (carry set, A = 0 after the INC).
  Entry: as CMD_REN.
  Exit: as CMD_REN.
  Error: as CMD_REN.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .342F Carry: directories

@3430 CMD_MOVE
  The MOVE command: A = 0 (Z) makes CMD_REN move. The operand of LD B,$37 at $3431 is SCF,
  the RNDIR command's entry ($3432: REN for directories).
  Entry: as CMD_REN.
  Exit: as CMD_REN.
  Error: as CMD_REN.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .3430 A = 0: move
  .3431 Its operand ($37) is SCF, RNDIR's entry

@3433 CMD_REN
  The REN and RENAME commands (A = $FF, NZ), MOVE, MVDIR and RNDIR: the first argument's
  matching entries are renamed (EXDOS function 9) to the second argument's name, or moved
  (function 10) to the directory the second argument names. "/H" takes hidden entries too.
  1. Takes the first argument apart and finds its first entry; the character the parse cut
     off is put back.
  2. A second request block (NEXT_REQ_BLOCK) on the same drive takes the second argument:
     a name to rename to (no path: B bit 1 clear), or a directory path to move to (parsed
     with its last element entered).
  3. Each matching entry: function 9 with BC = the second block, or 10 with BC = the path text.
  Entry: AF = the mode (Z: move; carry: directories); HL = the argument text; IX = the
  request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0 (C) after the last one.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code ($AE for a path in the new name, $9F for a missing argument).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .3433 The first name and "/H"
  .3439 Its first entry
  .343C HL = where the parse stopped
  .343F Put back the character the parse replaced
  .3441 The second request block
  .3444 Its drive = the first block's
  .344B HL' = the second block
  .344F The second argument
  .3452 C = 0: copy the name
  .3454 Rename?
  .3457 Move: enter the path's last element

@3459 REN_PARSE_NEW
  Takes the second argument apart into the second block, back to the first block.
  .345A The second argument
  .345D Nothing after it?
  .3461 IX = the first block again
  .3466 AF = the command's mode, AF' kept
  .346A Rename?
  .346C Move: HL' = the path text

@3472 REN_CHECK_NEW
  The new name may have no path (B bit 1) and must be given.
  .3472 A path in the new name?
  .3474 Then $AE (Invalid parameter)

@347A REN_MOVE_START
  BC = the result buffer, DE = the block.

@347D REN_MOVE_LOOP
  Function 9 for REN, 10 for MOVE.
  .347D AF = the command's mode
  .347E Function 10: move
  .3480 Move?
  .3482 Else function 9: rename

@3483 REN_MOVE_CALL
  Runs the function on the entry found, then finds the next.
  .3485 HL = the request block
  .3486 DE = the entry's descriptor
  .3488 BC = the second block or the path text
  .348C Rename or move it
  .348F Then the next one
  .3492 Until the search ends

@3495 CMD_VOL
  The VOL command: with a name, writes it as the drive's volume label (EXDOS function 6 on
  the raw name, IX+6 bit 3); without one, writes "Volume in drive A: is NAME" or "... has no
  name".
  Entry: HL = the argument text; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .3495 Read the name raw
  .349A Nothing after it?
  .34A0 A = the parse's flags

@34A1 VOL_LABEL
  Sets the volume label (A bit 0 set: a name given, function 6) or finds and writes it
  (function 4 on the root, from DIR too).
  Entry: A bit 0 = a name given; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .34A1 BC = the result buffer, DE = the block
  .34A4 A name?
  .34A6 Function 6: write it
  .34AB Function 4: find the label
  .34B0 $CF: no label
  .34B4 Another error?

@34B6 VOL_SHOW
  Writes "Volume in drive A:" and " has no name", or " is " and the 11-character name.
  .34B6 Keep the result
  .34B7 "Volume in drive "
  .34BC "A:"
  .34C0 No label?
  .34C1 " has no name"
  .34C6 " is "
  .34CA DE = the entry's name
  .34CF End it after 11 characters
  .34D4 The name
  .34D7 A new line

@34DA CMD_CLS
  The CLS command: writes code $1A and writes out the buffer.
  Entry: IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = EXOS's status, flags from it.
  Changes: AF.
  .34DA Code $1A

@34E2 CMD_ECHO
  The ECHO command: writes its argument text and a new line.
  Entry: HL = the argument text; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0 and Z.
  Error: NZ, A = EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL.
  .34E2 DE = the text

@34E9 CMD_TYPE
  The TYPE command: COPY to the default channel (bit 5 of IY-$2B clear).
  Entry: as COPY_TYPE.
  Exit: as COPY_TYPE.
  Error: as COPY_TYPE.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .34E9 Not COPY

@34EF CMD_COPY
  The COPY command (bit 5 of IY-$2B set); goes on at COPY_TYPE.
  Entry: as COPY_TYPE.
  Exit: as COPY_TYPE.
  Error: as COPY_TYPE.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .34EF COPY

@34F3 COPY_TYPE
  COPY and TYPE: a source and (for COPY) a destination, each a file specification, a
  "#n" channel or a device name ("NAME:").
  1. The source (PARSE_OPERAND) and its options from $36D4: "/A", and "/H" for a file.
  2. TYPE: the destination is channel $FF (B' bit 6 set). COPY: the second operand in the
     next request block and its options from $36DB ("/V", "/T", "/A" for a file, "/A" else).
  3. IY-$2A = the options of both. A file source is searched for (FIND_FIRST_ARG); a device
     is opened (OPEN_FREE_CHAN), its channel kept at IY-$2C.
  4. The copy itself from $3566 on.
  Entry: HL = the argument text; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, all alternates.
  .34F3 The source
  .34F8 Its options
  .34FE A name given?
  .3503 B' = the source's options
  .3506 HL = the text after it
  .3507 The second request block
  .350A The next argument
  .350E COPY?
  .3515 TYPE: B' bit 6
  .3517 C' = 0
  .3519 The destination: channel $FF
  .351F Nothing after it?

@3521 COPY_DEST
  COPY's destination: the second operand and its options.
  .3521 C = 0: copy the name
  .3522 The destination
  .3526 DE' = the text after it
  .3529 Its options

@352F COPY_ARGS_END
  Nothing may follow.

@3532 COPY_ARGS_DONE
  Back to the source block; IY-$2A = the options of both; a file source is searched for.
  .3532 IX = the source block
  .353B The options of both
  .3541 D = the source's first byte: $FF for a file
  .3544 A file?
  .3548 Its first entry

@354E COPY_SRC_CHAN
  The source is a channel or a device: refused with an all-"?" destination name ($AE);
  a device (D not 0) is opened, its channel kept at IY-$2C and the block's +2.
  .354E The destination's name all "?"?
  .3552 Then $AE (Invalid parameter)
  .3555 D = 0: a channel number
  .3559 DE = the device's name
  .355C Open it
  .3560 Close it at the end

@3566 COPY_VERIFY_OPT
  With "/V" (IY-$2A bit 7), IY-$29 = $FE when IY-$58 was not 0, else $FF, and IY-$58 is
  cleared.
  .356A /V?
  .3570 A = IY-$58
  .3575 A = $FF when it was not 0
  .3576 $FE or $FF
  .357A Clear it

@357E COPY_DEST_DEV
  A device destination (first byte not $FF or 0) is created (EXOS 2, $0339); its channel
  goes to IY-$2D and the block's +$62.
  .357E D = the destination's first byte
  .3582 A file ($FF) or a channel (0)?
  .3587 DE = the device's name
  .358A Create the channel
  .358E Close it at the end

@3594 COPY_SRC_OPEN
  A file source is opened (EXDOS function 12).
  .3594 BC = the result buffer, DE = the block
  .3597 HL = the block
  .3598 DE = the entry's descriptor
  .359A A file?
  .35A0 Function 12

@35A6 COPY_ALLOC
  Allocates up to four segments for the copy's buffer; HL' adds up DE from each EXOS 24.
  .35A6 HL' = 0
  .35AB HL = the segment list (IX-$133)
  .35B0 B = 4 tries

@35B2 COPY_ALLOC_LP
  One EXOS 24 (allocate a segment).
  .35B5 A = the segment
  .35B7 Got one?
  .35B9 A shared segment: its boundary
  .35BD None: segment 0, size 0

@35C0 COPY_ALLOC_STO
  Stores the segment and adds DE to HL'.
  .35C0 The segment number

@35D2 COPY_BUF_SIZE
  The buffer's size, rounded down to an even page, at most $FE00; without a segment, EXDOS
  function 30's scratch buffer (512 bytes) is used.
  .35D2 L = 0
  .35D4 Any space?
  .35D8 No segments
  .35DD Function 30: the scratch buffer
  .35E4 Its address in place of the kept word
  .35E6 512 bytes

@35E9 COPY_TYPE_CAP
  TYPE moves at most $2000 bytes at a time.
  .35E9 COPY?
  .35EF $20 pages at most

@35F5 COPY_FILE_LP
  Writes the source's name for a copy out of a named directory ($36E2), checks the stop
  ($2F23), points IX at the destination block and, for a file destination, creates it:
  $3884, code $1A at DE, with "/A" EXDOS function 16 ($3862), then function 13.
  .35F7 The name, when copying a directory's files
  .35FA Stop pressed?
  .3601 HL = IY
  .3605 DE = IY+$18D
  .360B IX = the destination block (IY+$15D)
  .360E A file ($FD-$FF)?
  .3613 Already open?
  .361D C = 1
  .361F Code $1A
  .3622 /A?
  .362A Function 13: create the file

@362F COPY_DEST_CHK
  An error goes to $3699; with the stop status kept from $2F23, the new file is deleted
  (function 8).
  .3631 The kept status
  .3633 Not stopped?
  .3635 HL = the destination block
  .3638 Function 8: delete it

@36E2 COPY_SHOW_NAME
  When the source was a directory named on the command line (IX+$13 bit 5), writes the
  entry's name and a new line.
  Entry: IX = the source block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: NZ, A = EXOS's status.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AF', DE'.
  .36E5 Destination +$14 = the block's high byte
  .36E8 A directory's files?
  .36EE The compact name

@382E PARSE_OPERAND
  Takes one COPY or TYPE operand apart into the request block at IX: "#n" is a channel
  number, else a file specification (PARSE_ARG), or a device when the parse stops at ":"
  ($3DFA finds the name). A file ends at $3859, inside the JP NZ at $3858 (never taken: A
  is 0): XOR A, SCF, so carry marks a file.
  Entry: HL = the text; C = function 1's flags; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: carry for a file (IX+$13 = the parse's flags), NC for a channel or a device (IX+0 =
  the name's high byte or 0, IX+1 its low byte, IX+2 the channel, IX+$13 = 1); A = 0; IX+$14
  = D; DE = the text after it; AF' = the entry AF.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF', L'.
  Keeps: IX.
  .382F A "#"?
  .3832 The channel number
  .3837 L' = the first character
  .383A A file specification?
  .383D A = the character that ended it
  .383E A device name?
  .3842 Put back the ":"
  .3846 And the first character
  .3847 Find the device's name
  .384A Its low byte

@384D OPERAND_CHAN
  A channel or device operand: IX+0/IX+1 = DE (the name, or 0 and the channel), IX+2 = E.
  TYPE's destination enters here with DE = $00FF.
  Entry: DE = the device's name, or D = 0 and E = the channel; HL = the text after it; IX =
  the request block.
  Exit: A = 0, Z, NC; C = the next character; DE = the text; IX+$13 = 1; IX+$14 = D.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  .384D The channel number
  .3850 The name's high byte, 0 for a channel
  .3853 C = the next character
  .3854 DE = the text
  .3856 B = 1, the mark for IX+$13
  .3858 Never taken (Z): its operand ($AF $37) is the file path's XOR A, SCF
  .385B IX+$14 = D
  .385E IX+$13 = B

@3862 FN16_SETUP
  Runs EXDOS function 16 ($3864 on) with IX = BC' and IY-$80 = 3; status $E4 becomes 0.
  Entry: BC = passed on; BC' = the block for IX; IX = the request block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z (also for $E4, with A' = $E4 and NZ).
  Error: NZ, A = the error code.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF'.
  .3862 Function 16

@3884 DE_FROM_BC_ALT
  DE = BC', then $3888.
  Entry: BC' = the value.
  Exit: as $3888.
  Changes: AF, B, DE; as $3888.
  .3887 DE = BC'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Questions and notes

question 3858 What is IX+$14 (set to D, the text pointer's high byte, at $385B) for: only bit 7 is tested ($3613)?
question 35A6 Does EXOS 24 return DE as the space a segment gives, so that HL' is the buffer size?
question 3566 Is IY-$58 EXDOS's verify flag, saved and cleared by COPY's "/V"?
question 3D0B Is the request block always $60 bytes, so that COPY's two blocks and REN's are at IY+$FD and IY+$15D?

feedback missing 327F-34EF The command handlers' entry state comes from $2C47-$2C55 (A = $FF with NZ, NC; HL = the text; IX = DE = IY+$FD; C = 0): a card for a table-entered handler should carry it
feedback pattern 3858-385A JP NZ,nn after XOR A (and JP Z after OR $AE at $3D5A) is never taken and hides an entry in its operand; the card calls it "jumps out of the page"
feedback listing 3118-3131 Had to read $30D3-$3118 to see what $3127's POP takes (the number pushed at $30D3)
feedback listing 2B9B-2C58 Had to read the command dispatcher and decode the command table by hand to know which handler is which command
